            <ArticleTitle>Update on the use of corticosteroids in third molar surgery: systematic review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e342-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.oooo.2012.02.027</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2212-4403(12)00235-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Third molar extraction produces inflammation, pain, and trismus, and different pharmacologic therapies have been evaluated for the minimization of postsurgical symptoms. The most frequently prescribed drugs are nonsteroidal anti-inflammatories, followed by steroids and opiates.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A systematic search of the literature was carried out in PubMed, Scopus, MEDLINE, and Cochrane using steroid and third molar as key words.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The most relevant randomized controlled trials and meta-analyses (n = 28) were selected from among 72 articles.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Administration of corticosteroids improves the postoperative experience of patients and has a significant impact on trismus and inflammation. Greater effects appear to be achieved by using the parenteral route and by administering the corticosteroid before the surgery.</AbstractText>
